  I agree completely.  I have yet to ascend even one character, but I have
made it fairly far along with various classes (before dying a horrible
screaming death).  When you start out, each class is distinct and seperate. 
The further you go, the more they all look the same (with the exception of
the Priest, IMO, simply because of the ability to see if something is
cursed, uncursed or blessed just by seeing it).
  Some ideas I have:
  Let Wizards cast spells with NO food use.  Just Power, but make them fight
hamd-to-hand at a disadvantage.
  
  Give Knights superior weapon-use skills.  i.e., they automatically do more
damage and have a greater chance to hit with any given weapon.

  Barbarians could have a 10% chance of instantly killing any opponet:
  You see an opening, and pull the arms off the Wizard of Yendor! -- MORE --
  The Wizard of Yendor is killed!

  Cavemen might start out with a natural AC of 5, with a natural ability to
scare monsters, perhaps via the #monster command:
  #monster
  Direction?  [presses a direction]
  You scream in rage at the Dwarf! -- MORE --
  The dwarf drops everything in terror and flees!

  The Priest already has a useful skill that lasts until end-game, but maybe
throw in the ability to bless things.  They can bless at any time, adding 50
turns to the prayer counter.  They could bless everything they came across
if they so desired, but then they wouldn't get to sucessfully pray much. 
(They already have #turn, too, which is useful, or at least a lot of fun. 
The developer of the Priest class had the right idea.)

  Stuff like that.  Then, even when you're down to the bottom, with heaps of
equipment, every class STILL has something special that no other class could
hope to gain or even simulate.

  Samurai.... Hmmm... Maybe make their kick even better than it is.  So it
has a 10% chance of automatically killing any opponet.  :)
  You kick the nymph!  You knock her block right off!  -- MORE --
  You killed the nymph!

  Ooooh, that's evil.  I like it.  :)


